<pre>Terms and Conditions for Food Waste Management

1. Scope and Purpose

These terms aim to define the guidelines and responsibilities for effectively managing food waste to minimize environmental, social, and economic impact.



2. Segregation of Waste

All food waste must be segregated at the source into organic and non-organic categories.

Contamination of food waste with other waste types is strictly prohibited.



3. Storage and Collection

Proper storage containers must be used to store food waste. Containers should be sealed to avoid odors and pests.

Food waste should be collected regularly, adhering to the designated schedule.



4. Reduction and Reuse

Efforts must be made to reduce food waste at the source by monitoring consumption and avoiding overproduction.

Edible surplus food must be repurposed, donated, or shared wherever possible before disposal.



5. Composting and Recycling

All organic food waste should be directed to composting facilities to promote sustainable recycling.

Recycling initiatives for food packaging and other waste materials should be implemented alongside food waste management practices.



6. Prohibited Practices

Dumping food waste in open areas, water bodies, or unauthorized sites is strictly prohibited.

Burning food waste without prior approval is not allowed.



7. Legal and Environmental Compliance

All waste management practices must comply with local environmental laws and regulations.

Violations of waste disposal rules may result in penalties or legal action.



8. Awareness and Education

Staff, stakeholders, and consumers should be educated on food waste reduction and proper disposal practices.

Regular training and workshops must be conducted to ensure adherence to waste management protocols.



9. Monitoring and Reporting

Food waste management activities must be monitored regularly to track performance and identify areas for improvement.

Accurate records of food waste disposal and recycling must be maintained.



10. Collaboration and Partnerships

Collaboration with waste management agencies, NGOs, and community organizations is encouraged to enhance the effectiveness of food waste management initiatives.



11. Amendments and Updates

These terms and conditions are subject to periodic review and updates to align with new regulations or technological advancements.
